      "knowledge_point": "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3y),$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3y),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+h+3y).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+h+3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3y)}{h}).\n$$",
